WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Can one pixel create crossings in both directions?

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:18.000
One wet cell joins four branches immediately.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Assign each pixel a score. Add it to the pond mask when its score reaches the cutoff.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:47.000
Lowering the cutoff adds wet pixels. Equal scores enter in a single batch.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:59.000
The occupied fraction gains one cell. Connectivity jumps.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:13.000
A camera can change both pixel scores and the grid, shifting the first crossing.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:24.000
Hold one synthetic field fixed. Each observation starts from that same field.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:54.000
Lower the score cutoff together. Each view recomputes its mask and spanning flags.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:05.000
At this fixed score, the coarse view spans in both directions while the latent reference does not.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:16.000
The yellow and pink routes follow adjacent pond pixels between opposite edges.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:28.000
At another score, the boundary-texture observation reverses the verdict in the other direction.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:38.000
The observed pond fraction rises from about 0.634 to 0.650 while spanning is lost.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:49.000
The study targets the occupied fraction at the first span in both directions.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:03:04.000
Find the threshold by exact pixel ranking, then compare each estimate with the latent reference.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:19.000
The fixed-score examples expose the mechanisms. These markers quantify first-spanning fractions.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:28.000
The study operates on scalar scores before thresholding them into pond and ice.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:41.000
Replace each score with the local maximum over nine cells, then apply the same cutoff.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:58.000
Follow the window maximum. An ice pixel is added when its window contains a pond pixel.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:10.000
Now replay a real pair from the synthetic holdout. Both observed grids are 256 × 256.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:24.000
Only the one-pixel dilation changes. Pale green identifies newly labelled water.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:38.000
This example shows the mechanism. Next, apply the same two settings to all 48 fields.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:49.000
The holdout has 48 latent fields in each of three synthetic morphology families.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:05:03.000
Six paired observations per field make 864 stacks from 144 latent fields.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:20.000
Raw spanning has the lowest pooled median error, while its 90th percentile exceeds 0.05.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:34.000
Lay out the 48 Matérn fields. Horizontal position measures absolute error in the threshold.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:48.000
Each line pairs the same field. Only the outward one-pixel boundary displacement changes.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:06:03.000
Among the same 48 fields, the count with error at most 0.05 falls from 46 to 5.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:18.000
Each stratum requires median error ≤0.02, 90th-percentile error ≤0.05, and failures ≤5%.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:31.000
Raw spanning passes nine strata. None of the six methods passes all 18.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:45.000
Even an exact grid-spanning calculation can give a different verdict after observation.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:07:00.000
This threshold belongs to the image grid and depends on how that grid was observed.
